  "reasoning": "The patient presents with a burning pain in the stomach that comes and goes, accompanied by gas, bloating, weight loss, and loss of appetite. Most critically, the presence of dark, tarry stools (melena) and blood in the vomit (hematemesis) indicates upper gastrointestinal bleeding. These symptoms collectively point strongly toward an ulcerative process in the stomach or duodenum. Among the provided options, peptic ulcer disease is the only diagnosis that classically explains this specific combination of chronic burning epigastric pain, dyspepsia (gas/bloating), weight loss due to food avoidance, and significant GI bleeding. This aligns perfectly with reference cases [1], [18], and [20], which map similar stomach pain, appetite changes, and bloating to peptic ulcer disease. Other conditions like gastroesophageal reflux disease typically present with heartburn and regurgitation without frank GI bleeding, while typhoid and jaundice involve fever and systemic signs not described here. Therefore, peptic ulcer disease is the definitive diagnosis.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]peptic ulcer disease[/DIAGNOSIS]"
